# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""English translations, batch 7 (40 figures: tribal sons, church history, Trinity)."""
EN = {
"亚设（雅各之子）": dict(lesson="God's blessing gives us a surplus to bless others",
 summary="Jacob's eighth son (born of Zilpah), of whom Jacob prophesied he would yield rich food fit for kings; his inheritance was the fertile coastland of northwest Canaan; the New Testament prophetess Anna was of the tribe of Asher, who saw the infant Jesus in the temple and praised God.",
 witness="Jacob said: Asher's food shall be rich, providing delicacies for kings. Giving out of abundance is the passing on of blessing. Centuries later, the tribe of Asher produced an eighty-four-year-old prophetess, Anna, who waited for the Messiah in the temple—from bodily abundance to spiritual expectation, the tribe's blessing was realized in her in another way.",
 follow=["Blessed by Jacob to 'yield rich food and provide delicacies fit for a king,' becoming a source of provision and blessing (Gen 49:20)","His descendant the prophetess Anna of the tribe of Asher, serving in the temple day and night and recognizing the infant Jesus, witnessing the tribe's spiritual faithfulness (Luke 2:36-38)"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["The abundance God gives you is meant to make you a source of blessing to others","Anna was still waiting in the temple at eighty-four—does your waiting have such depth of commitment?"],
 prayer="Lord, Asher's blessing went from rich produce to spiritual waiting. Help me not only enjoy the abundance You give me, but become a source of blessing to others, and wait on You continually like Anna. Amen."),
"以萨迦（雅各之子）": dict(lesson="A secure inheritance must beware of trading it for the obedience of ease",
 summary="Jacob's ninth son (born of Leah), of whom Jacob prophesied he was like a donkey lying down among the sheepfolds, who saw the land was good and bent his shoulder to bear burdens, becoming a forced laborer; but the tribe of Issachar later produced two hundred leaders who understood the times and knew what Israel ought to do—a symbol of discernment.",
 witness="Jacob's prophecy has two sides: one, he saw the land was good and gladly bore burdens; the other, Scripture later praised the men of Issachar as 'understanding the times.' A settled person who stays alert can become the wisest leader; one who only enjoys comfort becomes a donkey content with forced labor.",
 follow=["Producing among his descendants two hundred leaders who understood the times (1 Chr 12:32)"],
 caution=["Possibly submitting willingly out of craving for a secure inheritance","The danger of trading comfort for servitude"],
 applications=["Does your stable situation make you more alert, or more slack?","'Understanding the times' is Issachar's legacy—how clear are you on the present spiritual situation?"],
 prayer="Lord, Issachar had a secure inheritance, yet had to beware of becoming a donkey content with ease. Help me stay alert in stability, becoming one who understands the times and knows what to do. Amen."),
"西布伦（雅各之子）": dict(lesson="A position at the crossroads is a mission opportunity God placed",
 summary="Jacob's tenth son (born of Leah), of whom Jacob prophesied he would dwell by the sea and become a haven for ships; his inheritance was in northwest Canaan near the Mediterranean, a crossroads of commerce and culture; his inheritance was also in Galilee, connected to Isaiah's Messianic prophecy.",
 witness="A position at the crossroads means you will meet all kinds of people. Jacob said he would dwell by the sea and become a haven for ships—a symbol of openness to the outside. Deuteronomy says they would sacrifice on the mountain, inviting all nations. People at the border, if faithful, are the most natural missionaries.",
 follow=["Jacob's prophecy that he 'will dwell by the seashore and become a haven for ships,' his inheritance a passage for trade and the coming and going of peoples (Gen 49:13)","Moses' blessing 'rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out,' still holding to the service of God at a cultural crossroads (Deut 33:18)","His Galilee inheritance later becoming where Jesus ministered and 'a great light shone' (Isa 9:1-2)"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Is your 'crossroads'—workplace, community—a place God put you to influence more people?","Being at the border is not a threat, but a mission opportunity"],
 prayer="Lord, Zebulun's position at the crossroads was no accident. Help me, at my crossroads, become a bridge bringing people to You, rather than being swallowed by various cultures. Amen."),
"便雅悯（雅各之子）": dict(lesson="The smallest often carries the heaviest grace",
 summary="Jacob's youngest son, whose mother Rachel died in childbirth; in Joseph's story he became the central bargaining chip, and Judah willingly offered to stay in his place; Jacob called him a ravenous wolf; Paul took pride in being of the tribe of Benjamin.",
 witness="He was the youngest, born under the shadow of his mother's death. Jacob earnestly guarded him. His brothers used him as a chip; Judah willingly suffered in his place. Yet this tribe produced Israel's first king Saul, and the New Testament's greatest apostle Paul. The most marginal often becomes the turning point of God's history.",
 follow=["A witness to Judah's act of repentance","The tribe producing King Saul and the apostle Paul"],
 caution=["The tribal prophecy: a ravenous wolf, needing a balance of strength and gentleness"],
 applications=["Do you feel you are 'the smallest,' unvalued? The tribe of Benjamin produced the apostle Paul","God often places the greatest potential in the least expected place"],
 prayer="Lord, Benjamin was the youngest, yet carried great grace. Help me not despise Your plan for me because I seem the smallest. Amen."),
"玛拿西（约瑟长子）": dict(lesson="God's choosing does not follow human birth order",
 summary="Joseph's firstborn; when Jacob crossed his hands at the deathbed blessing, his right hand rested on the younger Ephraim's head rather than the firstborn Manasseh's—another example of God choosing against the expected order.",
 witness="Joseph corrected his father: not so, my father! This is the firstborn. Jacob said: I know, my son, I know. God's choosing does not follow our expectations. Manasseh was not abandoned, only differently ranked. God sometimes chooses the younger over the elder, not because the elder is unimportant, but to display His sovereignty.",
 follow=["Though not in the order of the elder grandson, still blessed by Jacob, his tribe receiving vast inheritance on both sides of the Jordan"],
 caution=["Jacob crossing his hands to lay his right hand on the younger Ephraim's head, reminding us God's choosing often does not follow the order people assume (Gen 48:14,19)"],
 applications=["Have you ever thought you should be the 'firstborn,' only to find God had another arrangement?","God's choosing is not a denial of you, but a display of His sovereignty"],
 prayer="Lord, Manasseh was the firstborn, yet not the one chosen for first place. Help me accept Your order rather than insist on my own expectations, trusting Your arrangement is better than my expectation. Amen."),
"以法莲（约瑟次子）": dict(lesson="Chosen by God unexpectedly, respond with humility",
 summary="Joseph's second son, on whose head Jacob crossed his hands to lay his right, giving the greater blessing; his tribe became the core of the northern kingdom, so much so that 'Ephraim' became a synonym for the north; but the prophets Hosea and Isaiah repeatedly warned of Ephraim's pride and apostasy.",
 witness="He was the younger yet received the greater blessing—pure grace, with no merit at all. The problem is that those shown the most grace are sometimes the most proud. The tribe of Ephraim later became self-important, arrogantly thinking all others should pledge allegiance to them. Being chosen is grace; taking pride in being chosen is the start of a fall.",
 follow=["Receiving Jacob's special blessing of the crossed right hand","His tribe becoming the main force of the northern kingdom of Israel"],
 caution=["The tribe of Ephraim later known for tribal pride (Judg 12:1; Isa 9:9)","'Ephraim' becoming a synonym for the north's apostasy"],
 applications=["Has the greatest grace you received become a reason for your pride?","Being chosen is a calling, not a privilege—this is Ephraim's lesson"],
 prayer="Lord, Ephraim received the greatest blessing, yet turned to pride. Help me return all the grace You gave me to Your glory, rather than letting grace become fuel for pride. Amen."),
"罗得": dict(lesson="Shortsighted choices bring long-term costs",
 summary="Abraham's nephew, who when parting from Abraham chose by sight the fertile but sinful plain of Sodom, and was finally rescued by angels before the city's destruction, yet lost his wife and all his possessions—a warning of choosing by the lust of the eyes.",
 witness="He lifted up his eyes and saw the whole plain of the Jordan was well watered, and chose it—he chose by his eyes. It was fertile, prosperous, the best. He did not ask God, did not seek wisdom, only saw. Years later he lost his wife there, lost everything. A choice by the eyes is not a choice by wisdom.",
 follow=["Rescued by God's mercy before judgment came","Abraham once prayed to intercede for him"],
 caution=["Making a major choice by the lust of the eyes rather than God's will","Being eroded by long residence in a morally corrupt environment","Becoming accustomed to Sodom's sin, losing discernment"],
 applications=["Was your recent major choice made by sight or by prayer?","After long staying in a sinful environment, has your discernment quietly declined?","God's mercy often surpasses our bad choices—but the cost still remains"],
 prayer="Lord, Lot chose Sodom by sight, and I often do the same. Help me come before You before important decisions, judging direction by Your eyes rather than the lust of the eyes. Amen."),
"罗得的妻子": dict(lesson="Looking back is the most fatal disobedience to God's command",
 summary="Fleeing Sodom, she was clearly commanded by the angels not to look back, yet looked back during the escape and at once became a pillar of salt. Jesus Himself cited her as a warning: remember Lot's wife.",
 witness="There was only one command: do not look back. She looked back. Scripture does not explain why—nostalgia? curiosity? reluctance? Whatever it was, that one look back ended everything. God's command needs no reason, only obedience. Jesus said: remember Lot's wife—these words span thousands of years, still asking us: where is your heart?",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Still looking back before a clear command","Her heart still longing for the life about to be judged","Outwardly fleeing, inwardly never having left"],
 applications=["When leaving some old life or sin, does your heart still look back?","'Do not look back' is not only physical direction, but the direction of the heart","Jesus said remember Lot's wife—where is your heart today?"],
 prayer="Lord, Lot's wife lost everything the moment she looked back. Help me, where You say do not look back, have courage to bid a thorough farewell to the old life—not only leaving physically, but turning my heart fully forward. Amen."),
"罗得的女儿们": dict(lesson="Solving problems with wrong means in desperation brings long-term calamity",
 summary="After escaping Sodom, the two daughters in despair schemed to make their father drunk and lay with him, bearing the ancestors of the Moabites and Ammonites respectively—a tragedy of solving problems by illicit means in extreme circumstances.",
 witness="They grew up in the most morally corrupt city; Sodom's ways had become their framework of thinking. Facing desperation, they devised a Sodom-style solution. Their problem was real—the family line seemed about to end—but their method brought thousands of years of calamity. Solving real problems with wrong methods is sin's most common entry point.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Solving problems with a Sodom-style way of thinking","Abandoning God's standard for a quick fix in desperation","Bringing forth Moab and Ammon, two nations long hostile to Israel"],
 applications=["Have you, under great pressure, used the excuse 'just this once' to do something against God's standard?","Environment shapes thinking patterns—how is your long-term environment shaping your decision-making?"],
 prayer="Lord, Lot's daughters used the wrong method in desperation. Help me, in the most desperate situation, still trust You have a way that accords with Your will, rather than designing my own shortcut. Amen."),
"俄珥巴": dict(lesson="The rational choice and the choice of faith lead to different ends",
 summary="Ruth's sister-in-law, a Moabite woman who, when Naomi told them to return to their families, kissed her mother-in-law in tears and returned to her own people, forming a sharp contrast with Ruth. Her choice was rationally beyond reproach, yet parted ways from God's historical trajectory.",
 witness="She wept too; she loved Naomi too. But she went back. There was no right or wrong in this choice—Naomi herself told them to return. Orpah made a rational, safe, reasonable choice. Ruth made a crazy, unsecured, beyond-reason choice. The two paths parted, and history remembered only Ruth. Sometimes the choice of faith is more meaningful than the rational choice.",
 follow=["Having real love and reluctance toward her mother-in-law"],
 caution=["Choosing safety over faith at the critical moment","Missing God's history of redemption"],
 applications=["Have you, before some calling, made a 'rationally correct' choice that missed God's will?","Orpah's choice was not wrong, but Ruth's choice entered history—the choice of faith often surpasses rational calculation"],
 prayer="Lord, Orpah's choice was rationally sound, yet missed Your plan. Help me, at life's critical crossroads, not only calculate rationally, but seek Your will, with Ruth-like courage of commitment. Amen."),
"米迦（以法莲人）": dict(lesson="Worshipping God your own way equals worshipping yourself",
 summary="A man of the hill country of Ephraim who privately cast idols and set up a household shrine, hiring a Levite as his private priest, thinking he would thus gain God's blessing; later the idols were seized by the tribe of Dan, the priest taken too, and everything came to nothing.",
 witness="He said: now I know the LORD will bless me, because I have this Levite as priest. He designed a worship system for himself, worshipping God in the way he thought right, yet never asked what God wanted. In the end the idols were seized, the priest left, everything vanished. Worshipping God your own way is not worshipping God, but worshipping a god of your own design.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Casting idols on his own, against the second commandment","Thinking having a Levite priest equaled God's approval","Instrumentalizing faith: thinking that having the religious form meant having God's blessing"],
 applications=["Do you have your own designed 'way of faith' that has never actually asked what God wants?","A complete religious form does not equal God's presence—Micah had a priest and idols, and in the end had nothing"],
 prayer="Lord, Micah worshipped his own way, thinking he had gained You. Help me not turn faith into my own religious system, but truly seek You and obey the worship You require. Amen."),
"利未人与他的妾（士师记）": dict(lesson="A morally collapsing society begins with each person's weakness",
 summary="The darkest event in Judges: a Levite's concubine fled to her family; he brought her back, and passing through Gibeah, a mob that night demanded violence; he pushed her out the door, and she was abused to death. He dismembered her body to send a signal, igniting an Israelite civil war that nearly wiped out the tribe of Benjamin. The most brutal footnote to Judges' 'everyone did as he saw fit.'",
 witness="That night, he pushed his concubine out the door—Scripture says he pushed her out. He protected himself and sacrificed her. He later dismembered the body and waged war in the name of anger, yet never took responsibility for her death. In the whole event, not one person truly acted by God's standard. Judges ends its darkest story saying: in those days there was no king in Israel; everyone did as he saw fit.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Sacrificing the weak to protect himself in crisis","Politicizing tragedy rather than facing his own responsibility","The total collapse of morality—the whole society lost God's standard"],
 applications=["Under the greatest pressure, do you protect the most vulnerable, or yourself first?","'Everyone did as he saw fit' is the endpoint of a society without God, and the sum of every individual's choices"],
 prayer="Lord, the darkness at the end of Judges reminds me how deep people can fall without Your standard. Help me hold to Your justice and mercy in this age, becoming one who is light in the darkness. Amen."),
"以笏": dict(lesson="God uses unexpected people to accomplish seemingly impossible tasks",
 summary="The second judge of Israel, left-handed, who by a carefully planned action assassinated Eglon king of Moab, who had oppressed Israel for eighteen years, and led Israel to eighty years of rest.",
 witness="He was left-handed—in that era seen as a defect—yet hid his sword on his right thigh, and the guards did not search there. God uses not only perfect people, but those willing to obey, those considered flawed. The unexpected is exactly God's style of working.",
 follow=["His left-handed 'disadvantage' becoming the very means God used, hiding the sword on his right thigh to evade the search, assassinating the Moabite king who oppressed Israel eighteen years (Judg 3:16-21)","Carefully planning, single-handedly bearing a high-risk mission, afterward sounding the trumpet to rally the people in pursuit, bringing eighty years of rest (Judg 3:27-30)"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Which of your 'defects' or 'disadvantages' might actually be where God uniquely equipped you?","Is there something God called you to do that you haven't started because it looks too hard?"],
 prayer="Lord, Ehud reminds me: You do not choose people by the world's standards. Help me not shrink back because of my weaknesses, trusting You can use the real me to fulfill Your calling. Amen."),
"珊迦": dict(lesson="Serve God where you are, with the tool already in your hand",
 summary="The third judge of Israel, who killed six hundred Philistines with an oxgoad and saved Israel—the most briefly recorded yet most powerful judge in Scripture.",
 witness="The whole record is only one verse. No backstory, no vision from God, no great army. Just one man, an oxgoad, and six hundred enemies—and he won. God does not wait until you are fully equipped; God uses what is already in your hand.",
 follow=["Killing six hundred Philistines with an oxgoad to save Israel, the only farm tool in his hand becoming God's weapon (Judg 3:31)","Though recorded in only one inconspicuous verse, God still remembered and used him—the lowly person and tool are not overlooked by God"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["What is already in your hand? Don't wait until you're 'ready' to start","Where you are is your battlefield; don't wait for a better environment to serve"],
 prayer="Lord, Shamgar had only an oxgoad, yet with it changed Israel's destiny. Help me not despise what is in my hand, but bravely act for You where I am. Amen."),
"陀拉": dict(lesson="Quiet, faithful governance is also service God remembers",
 summary="A judge after Gideon, who quietly governed Israel for twenty-three years; Scripture records no battles or faults of his, only that he rose to save Israel.",
 witness="He had no great battles, no dramatic miracles, no lessons of failure. Only twenty-three years of quiet faithfulness. In God's record book, these twenty-three years are not forgotten. Sometimes the greatest service is the continuous guarding that receives no applause.",
 follow=["'Rose to save Israel,' quietly governing for twenty-three years after the chaos of the judges' cycle (Judg 10:1)","No legend of battles, no record of faults—long, stable guarding is itself a kind of faithfulness"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Do you undervalue your service because it isn't 'dramatic'?","God remembers all quiet faithfulness, not only the sensational"],
 prayer="Lord, You remembered Tola's twenty-three years of quiet guarding. Help me not be discouraged that my service receives no applause, trusting every faithful day is in Your record. Amen."),
"睚珥": dict(lesson="Blessing needs stewardship; power needs watchfulness",
 summary="A judge who governed Israel for twenty-two years, with thirty sons riding thirty donkeys and ruling thirty towns, a wealthy and prominent family, but Scripture gives no clear praise of his spiritual legacy.",
 witness="His record is full of numbers: thirty sons, thirty donkeys, thirty towns. This is the world's prosperity, not a spiritual legacy. Having much does not necessarily leave the most important thing.",
 follow=["Twenty-two years of stable governance of Israel"],
 caution=["The gap between the family's prosperity and spiritual influence","Abundant blessing needs more spiritual watchfulness"],
 applications=["What is the state of your family in material and spiritual terms?","The abundance God gives is for greater entrustment, not only for enjoyment"],
 prayer="Lord, Jair had thirty sons and thirty towns, yet left no spiritual witness. Help me, amid the abundance God gives, first build a spiritual legacy and not only a material one. Amen."),
"以比赞": dict(lesson="Doing your duty in your own post is pleasing to God",
 summary="A judge from Bethlehem who governed Israel for seven years, with thirty sons and thirty daughters, building a family network among the people, passing his term smoothly.",
 witness="Seven years, thirty sons, thirty daughters. His record is brief, but he provided stability in that turbulent era. Not every calling needs a dramatic story; sometimes doing your duty is the greatest contribution.",
 follow=["Serving as judge of Israel for seven years, bringing stable governance in the turbulent age of the judges","With thirty sons and thirty daughters in wide marriage alliances, building family and community networks, promoting connection between tribes"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["In your post, are you steadily doing your duty?","Not every service must be earth-shattering; faithfully completing what's entrusted is the foundation"],
 prayer="Lord, Ibzan was faithful for seven years in an ordinary post. Help me not despise ordinary commitment, being a reliable person in my post. Amen."),
"以伦": dict(lesson="Obscure faithfulness is also a foundation of God's kingdom",
 summary="A Zebulunite who governed Israel for ten years; Scripture's record of him is only two verses, no battles and no faults—one of the most silent judges.",
 witness="Two verses, ten years of service. No story, no famous sayings, no miracles. Only God knows what he did in these ten years. Sometimes the most faithful service stays in history's margins, yet is written on God's heart.",
 follow=["Serving as judge for ten years, recorded in only two verses, no battles and no faults—a silent, 'nameless' faithful servant","Reminding us: being used by God need not be sensational; quietly holding one's post has value in itself"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Are you willing to be one history doesn't remember, but God does?","Spiritual influence is not always measurable; faith continues even without seeing results"],
 prayer="Lord, Elon has only two verses, yet ten years of faithfulness. Help me not change direction for lack of applause, learning to be one You see and remember, even if the world forgets me. Amen."),
"押顿": dict(lesson="Family prosperity needs to be connected to a spiritual foundation",
 summary="A Pirathonite who governed Israel for eight years, with forty sons and thirty grandsons, all riding donkeys, a large and prominent family; Scripture records no spiritual legacy of his.",
 witness="Forty sons, thirty grandsons, all riding donkeys. This is a picture of family glory, yet missing God's name. The one with the most family members needs to think most seriously: what legacy am I leaving them?",
 follow=["Eight years of stable leadership of Israel"],
 caution=["The question mark between a large family and a spiritual legacy","Prosperity can obscure spiritual priorities"],
 applications=["What kind of legacy are you building for the next generation?","Which matters more—the size of the family or its spiritual depth?"],
 prayer="Lord, Abdon had a large family, yet left no spiritual witness. Help me, in building a family, always make a spiritual legacy the highest priority. Amen."),
"以利": dict(lesson="Indulging your sons is abandoning the responsibility God gave",
 summary="High priest of Israel, spiritually faithful and devout, yet because he tolerated his two sons Hophni and Phinehas sinning in the sanctuary, God rebuked him for 'honoring your sons above Me'; finally his sons died, the ark was captured, Eli himself fell and died, and his family's priesthood was stripped away.",
 witness="God said to him: why do you scorn My sacrifices and offerings, honoring your sons above Me? He was not ignorant; he simply did not discipline them. Knowing without acting is sometimes a heavier sin than not knowing. He loved his sons, but his love had no correction, only indulgence—this is not love, but abandonment.",
 follow=["Faithfully serving God for many years","Nurturing and guiding the young Samuel"],
 caution=["Knowing his sons sinned yet not seriously disciplining them","Honoring his sons above God","Failing to establish spiritual authority in the family"],
 applications=["Have you abandoned proper spiritual responsibility in your family for fear of hurting feelings?","Discipline is from love, and so is indulgence—but God clearly distinguishes which is which","In what relationships do you 'honor people above God'?"],
 prayer="Lord, Eli's tragedy reminds me: love is not indulgence. Help me, in my closest relationships, have courage to speak truth and act in true love by God's standard, not pleasing people with silence. Amen."),
"亚比亚他": dict(lesson="Faithful for many years yet taking the wrong side at the last step",
 summary="A faithful priest in David's time of trouble, the only one who escaped the massacre of the priests at Nob, who accompanied David through years of hardship, yet in old age supported Adonijah's bid for the throne rather than Solomon, and was finally deposed by Solomon, fulfilling God's prophecy against the house of Eli.",
 witness="He chose David in David's most dangerous period, paying everything. But his last choice was the wrong direction. Years of faithfulness cannot automatically guarantee the final choice is right. Every choice is a new choice; there is no coasting on past capital.",
 follow=["Risking his life to follow David in his hardship","Faithfully carrying the ephod to serve David for years"],
 caution=["In old age supporting Adonijah, standing against God's chosen Solomon","Past faithfulness cannot offset the final wrong choice"],
 applications=["A spiritual past cannot guarantee a spiritual future; every moment needs to choose God anew","Are you coasting on 'past capital' in some area, rather than renewing your commitment to God daily?"],
 prayer="Lord, Abiathar reminds me: no past faithfulness lets me skip choosing seriously today. Help me choose You anew each day, not coasting on past capital or inertia. Amen."),
"撒督": dict(lesson="Holding to loyalty toward God's appointed king amid political turmoil",
 summary="High priest in David's and Solomon's time; during Absalom's rebellion he chose to go with David, and when the ark was brought out he obediently sent it back to Jerusalem; during the contest for the throne he firmly supported God's chosen Solomon, and was finally greatly used as high priest in Solomon's court.",
 witness="During Absalom's rebellion, he carried the ark out of the city following David. David said to him: carry the ark of God back into the city... you are a seer; return to the city in peace. He obeyed, taking the ark back—this took greater faith, not heroic following, but quiet staying.",
 follow=["Unchanging loyalty to David in political crisis","Obeying David's command to send the ark back, trusting God would accomplish it","Supporting God's chosen Solomon"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Sometimes the greatest faithfulness is not heroic following, but quietly holding the position God gave you","Discern God's chosen direction amid chaos and firmly stand on that side"],
 prayer="Lord, Zadok's faithfulness was not by emotion but by discernment. Help me have Zadok's clarity in chaos—seeing God's hand, standing on the side God established, quietly and faithfully holding my post. Amen."),
"比拿雅": dict(lesson="Bravely executing the command is the highest expression of faithfulness",
 summary="Chief of David's thirty mighty men, who unarmed leaped into a pit and killed a lion, and single-handedly defeated an Egyptian warrior; later commander of Solomon's army, who when Solomon ordered him to execute Joab and Shimei faithfully carried it out without flinching.",
 witness="He was the kind of man you never need to doubt would carry out a command. Not because he lacked judgment, but because he knew who had authority and who was trustworthy. His courage was not only on the battlefield, but at the most dangerous political moments—ordered to execute a great figure like Joab, he carried it out all the same.",
 follow=["Brave and fearless, single-handedly killing a lion and a warrior","Consistent loyalty to David and Solomon","Not flinching to execute commands at the most dangerous moments"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["True courage is not the absence of fear, but acting despite fear","Faithfulness must pair with courage—knowing the right thing still needs the boldness to do it","Where do you need Benaiah-like courage: not only knowing, but executing?"],
 prayer="Lord, Benaiah's courage shows me the cost of faithfulness—not only choosing right, but having the boldness to do right. Help me not flinch where courage is needed, not make excuses, but faithfully carry out what You entrust. Amen."),
"亚希多弗": dict(lesson="Wisdom not centered on God must finally self-destruct",
 summary="David's most trusted counselor, called 'like inquiring of the word of God,' yet who betrayed David during Absalom's rebellion, and after his counsel was defeated by Hushai, hanged himself—a tragedy of talent and loyalty collapsing together.",
 witness="His counsel was so precise that Scripture says 'when one inquired of him, it was as inquiring of the word of God.' But his wisdom was not rooted in God, but in his own status and judgment. When his counsel was rejected, he realized defeat was certain, went home to put his affairs in order, and hanged himself—the wisdom of despair, not of hope.",
 follow=[],
 caution=["Betraying David, the master he had followed for years","Serving rebellion rather than God's will with outstanding wisdom","Killing himself the moment his counsel failed, with no room for repentance"],
 applications=["Are your wisdom and talents centered on God? Or on self-interest and judgment?","Ahithophel's tragedy: when your own judgment becomes the highest standard, failure means everything ends","True wisdom includes room for humility and repentance, not absolute self-certainty"],
 prayer="Lord, protect me from becoming Ahithophel—brilliant in wisdom yet self-centered. Help my talent and judgment always bow to Your will, rather than rise above You. Amen."),
"友阿爹": dict(lesson="Discord among co-workers harms the church's witness",
 summary="A woman believer of the Philippian church who had labored with Paul for the gospel, yet had a dispute with her co-worker Syntyche; Paul named them in his letter, asking them to reconcile in the Lord—one of church history's most famous cases of co-worker conflict.",
 witness="Paul did not avoid this conflict, but named them in a public letter and urged reconciliation. To be named by Paul as one who 'labored with me for the gospel' shows she truly served; but the dispute became a stumbling block to the witness. Discord among co-workers is no small matter; it can make the world misunderstand the gospel.",
 follow=["Having truly co-labored with Paul for the gospel"],
 caution=["Disputing with her co-worker Syntyche, affecting the church's witness","Requiring Paul to urge reconciliation in a public letter"],
 applications=["Is there a relationship in the church you need to reconcile? Take the first step today","Disputes among co-workers harm the church more than outside persecution; don't let private conflict obstruct the gospel"],
 prayer="Lord, protect my relationships with co-workers, that I may be reconciled in Christ, and let our unity be a witness to the gospel. Amen."),
"循都基": dict(lesson="Former co-workers needing reconciliation in the Lord because of a dispute",
 summary="A woman believer of the Philippian church who had labored with Paul for the gospel, yet had a dispute with her co-worker Euodia; Paul named them in Philippians, urging them to reconcile in the Lord—a classic NT case of church co-worker conflict.",
 witness="She and Euodia were both people who truly served, but the dispute meant they needed Paul's public mediation. Sometimes the more zealous the servant, the more easily friction arises in co-worker relationships—because both care, both have opinions. Paul said reconcile 'in the Lord,' reminding them the basis of reconciliation is not who's right, but their shared identity in the Lord.",
 follow=["Having labored with Euodia and Paul for the gospel, a contributing woman believer of the Philippian church (Phil 4:3)"],
 caution=["Having a dispute with her co-worker Euodia, so that Paul had to name them in his letter and urge them to be 'of the same mind in the Lord'","Discord among co-workers, if not promptly addressed, publicly harms the church's unity and witness"],
 applications=["The basis of reconciliation is 'in the Lord,' not who concedes or who wins","Is there a co-worker relationship you need to repair now?"],
 prayer="Lord, in conflict with co-workers let me first see my own part, reconciling on the basis of our shared identity in Christ. Amen."),
"乃缦的小女仆": dict(lesson="One word from a captive little girl changed a great general",
 summary="A captive Israelite girl, serving in the household of the Syrian general Naaman; learning her master had leprosy, she told her mistress about the prophet Elisha who could heal, finally leading to Naaman's healing and his coming to know Israel's God—one of Scripture's most moving cases of the great through the small.",
 witness="She was a victim of war—captured, enslaved, in a foreign land. She had every reason to stay silent, even to stand by. But she did not. She said: would that my master were with the prophet in Samaria. One sentence, from a nameless girl, set off the turning point of Naaman's whole life. God sometimes uses the smallest person to speak the most important words.",
 follow=["A captive slave in a foreign land, yet without bitterness, caring for her leprous master: 'would that my master were with the prophet in Samaria' (2 Kgs 5:3)","In the lowliest position, actively witnessing for God; one sentence led to the great general Naaman's healing and coming to know Israel's God"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Do you have a 'word' in your situation that you can speak to draw someone to God?","Even if you feel small and overlooked, God can still use one sentence of yours"],
 prayer="Lord, Naaman's little servant girl reminds me: You don't need me to have status or a name, only to be willing to speak. Help me not let opportunities slip by in my everyday situation. Amen."),
"尼布甲尼撒": dict(lesson="Humility after pride is the true knowledge of God",
 summary="King of Babylon who personally witnessed Daniel's three friends saved from the fire and Daniel interpreting dreams, yet was proud and arrogant, struck by God to live like a beast for seven years, finally humbly acknowledging the Most High God's sovereignty—the most dramatic case of repentance among Gentile kings.",
 witness="After seven years, he lifted his eyes to heaven, and his reason returned—he said: I bless the Most High and honor the One who lives forever. This is the truest theology spoken by a Gentile king. Pride at its extreme, and God used seven years of wilderness to bring him back. No one is too proud for God to deal with.",
 follow=["Finally humbly acknowledging the Most High God's sovereignty","Truly praising God after extreme humiliation"],
 caution=["Extreme arrogance: is this not great Babylon I have built by my mighty power?","Still exalting himself after witnessing God's wonders"],
 applications=["The peak of pride is closest to God's dealing—examine the area you're proudest of","Are Nebuchadnezzar's seven wilderness years your current situation? God is leading you toward humility","Being brought low from on high is not the end; the praise after humbling is the outcome God wants"],
 prayer="Lord, Nebuchadnezzar traded seven years for truly knowing You. Help me not wait until I'm beaten into the wilderness to be humble, but acknowledge before You today: all glory is Yours. Amen."),
"三位一体真神": dict(lesson="The one true God: Father, Son, and Spirit—same substance, same glory, same eternity",
 summary="Scripture reveals one God existing in three persons: the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit—same essence, same power, same glory, same eternity. They work together as one in creation, redemption, and sanctification. This entry lists no point to imitate, but remembers the wondrous works of the Triune God.",
 witness="'The LORD our God, the LORD is one' (Deut 6:4), yet baptizing into the one 'name' of the Father, Son, and Spirit (Matt 28:19). The Father sends, the Son accomplishes, the Spirit applies—the same God, three persons, at work in history and in your life and mine. Knowing His works gives worship a root and foundation.",
 follow=[],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Meditate on the works of the Triune God, turning worship from 'what can I get' to 'who God is and what God has done'","Experience the Trinity in prayer: in the Son's name, by the Spirit, to the Father (Eph 2:18)","Offer today one word of thanks each for creation, redemption, and indwelling grace"],
 prayer="One true God, Father, Son, and Spirit: I worship Your mighty power in creation, Your great love in redemption, Your great grace in indwelling guidance. Help me know who You are and what You have done, that my worship may no longer center on myself, but give glory to You alone. In the name of Jesus the Son, by the Spirit, to the Father. Amen."),
"Polycarp 坡旅甲": dict(lesson="Faithful unto death, never failing the Lord all his life",
 summary="A disciple of the apostle John, bishop of the church in Smyrna, who passed on the apostolic faith as a link between generations. Arrested at the age of eighty-six, pressed by the magistrate to curse Christ to save his life, he answered: 'I have served Him eighty-six years, and He has never wronged me; how can I blaspheme the King who saved me?' He was martyred by fire with composure.",
 witness="He lived out the simplest and most powerful pattern of 'faithful unto death'—the reality of a lifetime walking with the Lord left him without a single wavering word before death. This bishop, who had personal fellowship with the apostle John, passed on the pure faith he received unchanged to the next generation, and sealed that faith with eighty-six years of fidelity.",
 follow=["Walking with the Lord for eighty-six years, faithful to the end, not compromising under pressure","Receiving the apostle John's direct teaching, faithfully shepherding, a bridge of pure faith between generations","Facing the fire with calm composure, answering his persecutor's threat with 'the Lord has never wronged me'","Not using old age as a reason to exit, his love for the Lord shining all the brighter at the end","Burned for the faith, yet without fear, moving even his executioners"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Looking back over your years of faith, can you say like Polycarp, 'He has never wronged me'?","Age and seniority should not make one exit—the older, the more to shine for the Lord and pass pure faith to the next generation","When someone presses you to deny your faith, practice fidelity in small things first, so you stand firm when the great test comes","Find a young believer today and seriously pass on to them the true faith you received"],
 prayer="Lord, Polycarp served You eighty-six years and said 'You have never wronged me.' Make me too walk with You all my life, faithful to old age and death; when persecution and pressure come, give me courage to stand with composure, and to entrust the pure faith to the next generation. Amen."),
"Ignatius of Antioch 伊格那丢": dict(lesson="Willingly ground for the faith",
 summary="Bishop of the church in Antioch, escorted to Rome for execution (devoured by beasts). Along the way he wrote seven letters to various churches, emphasizing Christ's true divine-human natures, church unity, and the joy of martyrdom, calling himself 'the wheat of Christ, willing to be ground by the beasts into pure bread.'",
 witness="He regarded martyrdom as the highest honor of union with Christ, strengthening the churches by letters even unto death, turning suffering into shepherding.",
 follow=["Still shepherding the churches on the way to death, writing to strengthen believers","Calmly facing execution, viewing martyrdom as union with Christ","Firmly holding to Christ's true divine-human natures and church unity"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["In your own hardest, dying moment, do you wallow in self-pity, or still care for and strengthen others?","Martyrdom is not everyone's calling, but the heart that 'counts loss for the Lord as joy' can be learned by all","Hold to Christ's true divine-human natures—this concerns whether the salvation you believe is real","Write a letter or a word today to strengthen a brother or sister who is suffering"],
 prayer="Lord, Ignatius wrote to strengthen the churches even on his way to death. Give me such a heart—to care for others even in my own affliction, turning suffering into shepherding and blessing, holding fast to Your true divine-human natures unto death. Amen."),
"Justin Martyr 游斯丁": dict(lesson="Serving with reason, martyred for the true faith",
 summary="Originally a philosopher seeking truth, who after coming to the Lord wore a philosopher's robe and preached everywhere, writing the Apologies and the Dialogue with Trypho—the first-generation Christian apologist, who defended the faith before the Roman emperor and was finally martyred.",
 witness="He submitted his best learning to Christ, explaining the faith to the world by reason, proving that true faith withstands inquiry and is worth dying for.",
 follow=["Submitting philosophy and learning to Christ, using it to explain and defend the faith","Publicly pleading for truth before those in power, fearless of persecution","Learning and martyrdom together, word and deed consistent to the end"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Are your best learning and expertise also submitted at Christ's feet, used by Him?","Faith withstands questioning—don't fear thinking, but direct thinking toward Christ","Before those in power or skeptics, can you plead for what you believe in an orderly way?","Think today about how to use your area of expertise to glorify God and explain the faith to people"],
 prayer="Lord, Justin submitted philosophy and learning at Your feet, pleading for truth and being martyred for You. Make me too offer all I have learned to You, that I may both explain the faith by reason and be willing to pay the price for truth. Amen."),
"Jan Hus 扬·胡斯": dict(lesson="Rather burned than recant, igniting the spark of reform",
 summary="A Bohemian forerunner of reform, a century before Luther. He denounced church corruption and the selling of indulgences, holding that Scripture is above papal authority. Condemned as a heretic by the Council of Constance, he died by fire, singing and praying at the end. His martyrdom ignited the later Reformation.",
 witness="In an age of peak church power and isolation, he would rather be burned than deny the truth he believed, becoming a 'spark of reform.'",
 follow=["Holding Scripture as the highest authority, daring to challenge a corrupt system","Knowing he would die yet not retracting his beliefs, holding to conscience unto death","Still singing and praying in the fire, going to his death with composure"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["When the system or the majority conflicts with Scripture, who is your final authority?","When paying a price for what is right, Hus' example is 'rather burned than recant'","Only one whose conscience is bound by God's word can stand before coercion—what compromise does Scripture need to correct in you today?","The small fire you light may illuminate people a century later—faithfulness does not depend on seeing immediate results"],
 prayer="Lord, Hus, isolated and helpless, would rather be burned than deny the truth, still singing and praying in the fire. Give me a conscience that holds Scripture as the highest authority, standing firm under the pressure of system and majority, willing to be a spark of reform even if I never see the fruit in my lifetime. Amen."),
"William Tyndale 威廉·丁道尔": dict(lesson="Laying down his life so the common people could read God's word",
 summary="A pioneer who translated the Bible into common English, resolved that 'the plowboy would know Scripture better than the bishop.' Forced into exile on the continent to complete the New Testament translation, he was betrayed and arrested, strangled and his body burned. His dying prayer: 'Lord, open the King of England's eyes.' His translation became the foundation of later English Bibles.",
 witness="He traded his life for the common people's right to read God's word, and his dying request was not for his own salvation, but that his persecutor's eyes be opened.",
 follow=["Resolved that common people could understand Scripture, risking his life for it","Persisting to complete the translation work amid exile and persecution","Praying at the end for the king who persecuted him, holding a forgiving heart"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Do you treasure the blessing of freely reading Scripture in your own language? Someone traded their life for it","Pray for those who treat you unjustly or persecute you—Tyndale's dying request was 'open the King of England's eyes'","Bringing God's word to those who still cannot read or understand it is a mission still unfinished today","Read a passage of Scripture seriously today, remembering those who bled for it; don't take it for granted"],
 prayer="Lord, Tyndale laid down his life so common people could read Your word, and even at death prayed for the king who persecuted him. Make me treasure the Bible in my hands, hunger for Your word, and have his forgiving heart, praying for those who oppose me. Amen."),
"Jim Elliot 吉姆·艾略特": dict(lesson="Trading the perishable for the eternal is no folly at all",
 summary="A young American missionary who, with co-workers, brought the gospel to the isolated Auca people of Ecuador, and was killed at first contact, only twenty-eight. His famous saying: 'He is no fool who gives what he cannot keep to gain what he cannot lose.' His wife completed the work, and many of that people came to the Lord.",
 witness="With his young life he illustrated the value of giving all for Christ—giving up the perishable to gain the eternal.",
 follow=["Willingly giving up security, youth, and even life for the gospel","Valuing the eternal over earthly survival","His martyrdom becoming a door for the gospel—the sower fell, and the harvest grew"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["'He is no fool who gives what he cannot keep to gain what he cannot lose'—what are you trading this life for?","When giving up security for Christ, first ask: do I value the perishable or the eternal?","The sower falls, and the harvest may grow more—the fruit of faithfulness may not come when you can see it","Begin praying today for an unreached people or an unbeliever, asking God to raise up workers"],
 prayer="Lord, Jim Elliot laid down his youth and life for the eternal, saying it was no folly. Turn my values around, that I may value the eternal over the perishable, willing to give all for the gospel, trusting that even if I fall, You will make the harvest greater. Amen."),
"Richard Wurmbrand 理查德·沃姆布兰德": dict(lesson="Not forsaking faith in prison, speaking for the suffering after release",
 summary="A Romanian pastor imprisoned fourteen years under the communist regime, who endured torture yet did not abandon his faith, and prayed for his persecutors. After release he founded 'The Voice of the Martyrs,' wrote 'Tortured for Christ,' exposing to the world and serving the persecuted body of Christ.",
 witness="In the dungeon he experienced the Lord's presence; coming out, he held no grudge, but devoted the rest of his life to speaking for the suffering body.",
 follow=["Holding his faith through long torture and imprisonment, not betraying brothers","Praying for persecutors, overcoming hatred with love","Devoting the rest of his life to serving and speaking for the persecuted church"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["If one day your faith must cost you, will you practice fidelity in today's small things first?","Overcoming hatred with love—praying for those who persecute and wrong you was the lesson Wurmbrand learned in prison","Remember and practically support the body of Christ still persecuted today; don't let them be alone","In your comfort, ask God for a heart that suffers with the suffering body"],
 prayer="Lord, Wurmbrand experienced Your presence in dungeon torture, and after release prayed for his persecutors and spoke for the suffering body. Strengthen the persecuted church today, and give me a heart to suffer with them and intercede for them, that my comfort may not make me cold. Amen."),
"Irenaeus 爱任纽": dict(lesson="Holding the apostolic tradition to refute heresy",
 summary="A disciple of Polycarp (linked to the apostle John's lineage), bishop of Lyon. He wrote 'Against Heresies,' systematically defeating Gnosticism, establishing 'apostolic succession' and the authority of the canon, emphasizing the wholeness of salvation (incarnation, bodily resurrection), preserving the pure apostolic tradition.",
 witness="He did not win by novelty, but returned to 'the faith handed down from the apostles,' refuting flashy heresies with the orthodox foundation.",
 follow=["Using the faith handed down from the apostles as the standard to refute Gnostic heresy","Valuing the canon of Scripture and the whole salvation of incarnation and bodily resurrection","Defending the truth not by novelty but by returning to the foundation"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["Discerning true from false is not by novelty and flash, but by 'returning to the faith handed down from the apostles'","Facing plausible 'new teaching,' first ask: does this accord with Scripture and the apostolic foundation?","Salvation is whole—incarnation and bodily resurrection cannot be discarded; is the gospel you believe whole today?","Read a passage of the apostolic epistles today, letting the pure foundation correct the various claims you hear"],
 prayer="Lord, Irenaeus refuted flashy heresies with the faith handed down from the apostles. Root me in Your word and the apostolic foundation, not lured by every novel teaching, holding to the whole gospel. Amen."),
"Athanasius 亚他那修": dict(lesson="Standing alone against the world, holding to Christ's deity",
 summary="The standard-bearer of the Council of Nicaea, who fought all his life against the Arians who denied Christ's full deity. Exiled five times, nearly at odds with his whole age, he was called 'Athanasius contra mundum (Athanasius against the world).' He wrote 'On the Incarnation,' establishing the orthodox faith that 'the Son is of the same substance as the Father.'",
 witness="When the mainstream, the imperial power, and the majority all leaned toward heresy, he would rather stand alone, because truth does not depend on numbers.",
 follow=["Holding alone to the truth that 'Christ is fully God' when the whole world had strayed","Not compromising though exiled five times—truth does not shift with the majority","Positively expounding the foundation of salvation with 'On the Incarnation'"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["When the mainstream, the majority, and power all lean toward error, do you dare stand alone like Athanasius?","Truth does not depend on numbers—did you stand for a biblical position today despite isolation?","Christ is fully God—this is not a detail but the foundation of salvation; you must hold it","Pray today for those isolated for holding the truth, and ask God for this backbone"],
 prayer="Lord, when the whole world strayed, Athanasius stood against the world holding to Your Son's deity. Give me such courage and backbone, that when majority and power both lean toward error, I may still obey Your word alone, preferring isolation to compromising the truth. Amen."),
"Augustine of Hippo 奥古斯丁": dict(lesson="It is all God's grace",
 summary="From a dissolute youth to bishop of Hippo, his 'Confessions' records his journey to the Lord, and 'The City of God' established a Christian view of history. His doctrines of grace, original sin, and the church profoundly influenced Catholicism, the Reformed, and Lutheran traditions, and he is hailed as the most influential church father of the Western church. 'Our heart is restless until it rests in You.'",
 witness="He honestly laid bare his own corruption and the victory of God's grace, making 'it is all grace' the foundation of later generations' gospel.",
 follow=["Honestly facing his own sin and struggle, giving all glory to God's grace","Defending the truths of grace, original sin, and the church with profound thought","Pursuing all his life the rest of 'the heart resting in God'"],
 caution=["Some of his later views (e.g., on coercion) are disputed by later generations—even great people must be discerned by Scripture","'The heart is restless until it rests in God'—where does your heart rest today?"],
 applications=["Like Augustine, honestly face your own corruption, giving all victory to God's grace","'Our heart is restless until it rests in You'—where does your heart rest today?","Acknowledging 'it is all grace' means no longer earning God's acceptance by your own good works","Great people have blind spots too (e.g., his view on coercion)—read anyone with Scriptural discernment","Today, re-entrust to God's grace an area you've been striving in by your own effort"],
 prayer="Lord, Augustine honestly laid bare his sin and gave all glory to Your grace. Make my heart no longer wander, but rest in You alone; let me acknowledge it is all grace, not relying on myself, but boasting only in the salvation of Your cross. Amen."),
"Anselm 安瑟伦": dict(lesson="Faith seeking understanding",
 summary="The father of Scholastic theology, who proposed the 'satisfaction' theory of atonement (Christ's death satisfies the demand of God's justice) and the ontological argument for God's existence. His famous saying: 'faith seeking understanding (fides quaerens intellectum)'—believing first, then seeking to understand.",
 witness="He did not doubt first then believe, but 'I believe so that I may understand,' letting reason serve rather than override faith.",
 follow=["With the order of 'believe first, then seek to understand,' letting reason serve faith","Pondering deeply the meaning of redemption, expounding how Christ's death satisfies God's justice","Doing theology with a humble, devout heart"],
 caution=[],
 applications=["'Faith seeking understanding'—believe first, then seek to understand, letting reason serve rather than override faith","Facing a truth you don't understand, do you doubt first, or believe first and ask God to open your understanding?","Ponder deeply the meaning of redemption: how Christ's death satisfies God's justice, making you both reverent and grateful","Take a spiritual question you've long puzzled over, first bow in worship, then ask God to illumine you"],
 prayer="Lord, Anselm served You with 'faith seeking understanding,' letting reason humbly serve faith. Make me too trust first and then seek to understand, both reverent and grateful before the wonder of Your redemption, not letting my mind override Your revelation. Amen."),
}
